==============================================================================
 SLICE 2B - COUNTING DECLARATIONS WITH A VISITOR
==============================================================================

What it does:
  For a PARSED file, count what is written in it:
    class, interface, enum, record, annotation, anonymous class,
    method, constructor, executable, lambda
  A broken (PARSE_FAILED) file gets NO counts at all.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - THE VISITOR (a tour guide walking the tree)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The tree can be deep: a class inside a class inside a method...
We need to visit EVERY node exactly once.

JavaParser gives us VoidVisitorAdapter: a tour guide that already
knows how to walk every room (node) and every room inside it.
We only say what to do in the rooms we care about:

    @Override
    public void visit(MethodDeclaration node, Void arg) {
        methodCount++;                 // 1. do our job in this room
        super.visit(node, arg);        // 2. "guide, continue into the rooms inside"
    }

RECURSION: the guide enters a room, and for each room inside it,
does the same thing again. That is why nested classes work with no
extra code from us.

THE CLASSIC BUG - forgetting super.visit(...)
  Without it, the guide stops at that room and never goes inside.
  We TRIED this on purpose (real experiment):
    removed super.visit in the class visitor ->
    expected method 10 ... but was method 0
    3 tests failed. Then we put the line back: 45/45 pass.
  So our tests would catch this bug if anyone made it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - EXPLICIT vs IMPLICIT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Java secretly writes some code for you ("implicit"):
    record Point(int x, int y) {}   -> constructor, x(), y(), equals...
    enum Color { RED, GREEN }       -> values(), valueOf()
    class Empty {}                  -> a default constructor

CodePulse counts only what the PROGRAMMER WROTE ("explicit").
For the three lines above: methods 0, constructors 0.
Why? We measure the code people must read and review. Hidden
generated code is not something a reviewer reads.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 THE COUNTING RULES (Blueprint 9.3), with examples
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  class        named classes, INCLUDING nested and local ones
               NOT interfaces, NOT anonymous "new X() { }"
  interface    "interface Greeter" (an @interface is NOT an interface here)
  annotation   "@interface Audit" - its "String value();" is NOT a method
  anonymous    "new Runnable() { ... }" - counted separately, not a class
  method       every written method, INCLUDING abstract ones with no body
  constructor  written constructors, INCLUDING record compact ones: Item { ... }
  executable   methods and constructors that HAVE A BODY { ... }
                  "abstract int weight();"   method yes, executable NO
                  "default String greet() {}" method yes, executable YES
  lambda       "name -> ..." counted, but NOT an executable

The big fixture (Declarations.java) has a running tally written in
comments next to each line. Open it and follow along:
  class 2, interface 1, enum 1, record 1, annotation 1, anonymous 1,
  method 10, constructor 3, executable 11, lambda 2

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TEST-FIRST (red -> green), real run
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Wrote the fixture and counted by hand FIRST.
2. Wrote the tests with those hand numbers.
3. Ran them against an empty "stub" -> RED: 5 ERRORS.
   (Errors, not Failures: the stub CRASHED before comparing numbers.
    Remember Phase 0: Failure = wrong answer, Error = crash.)
4. Wrote the real visitor -> GREEN: all pass.
Why this order? The numbers come from YOUR head, not from the code.
If we ran the code first and copied its output, a bug would become
the "expected" answer.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BROKEN FILE = NO NUMBERS (the half-tree lesson, made concrete)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    class Half { void one() {} void two() {
The tree stops early. A half-tree might say "1 method" - wrong, but
it LOOKS real. So CodePulse gives NO counts:
    outcome.syntaxTree().map(DeclarationCounter::countIn)  -> empty
Test: aBrokenFileGetsNoCountsAtAllNotSmallerCounts

