==============================================================================
 SLICE 2F - COMPLEXITY (THE HEART OF PHASE 2)
==============================================================================

What is it?
  Roughly: HOW MANY DIFFERENT PATHS can a method take?
  More paths = more cases to test, more to think about when reviewing.
  It is a HINT for reviewers, not a bug predictor.

THE RULES (Blueprint 9.4) - learn these by heart for the gate:

  Start at 1                           (one straight path)
  +1  if                               (else-if is another if, so +1)
  +1  for, for-each, while, do-while
  +1  catch                            (catch (A | B e) is ONE catch)
  +1  ternary   cond ? a : b
  +1  &&   ||                          (anywhere, even in a return)
  +1  each switch case entry           case "a", "b" -> ...  is ONE entry
                                       old style  case 1: case 2:  is TWO
  +1  a "when" guard on a case         (and its && / || count too)
  +0  else, try, finally, return, throw, default, bitwise & |

  SKIPPED (separate units, measured on their own or not at all):
      lambda bodies, local classes, anonymous class bodies
  BUT the ARGUMENTS of  new Thread(a && b) { ... }  are evaluated in
  THIS method, so that && counts here.

  Abstract method (no body)  ->  complexity NULL, never 1.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 THE BLUEPRINT EXAMPLE, STEP BY STEP
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    int fee(boolean premium, int units) {          start      1
        if (premium && units > 10) return 0;       if +1  && +1
        for (int i = 0; i < units; i++) {          for +1
            if (i % 2 == 0) audit(i);              if +1
        }
        return 1;                                  return +0
    }                                              total      5

    "i < units" inside the for does NOT add: the for already counted.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW THE VISITOR DOES IT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Same tour-guide visitor as 2B, run on ONE method body at a time:
    visit(IfStmt)      -> complexity++ ; super.visit (keep walking)
    visit(BinaryExpr)  -> only if operator is AND or OR: complexity++
    visit(LambdaExpr)  -> do NOTHING, no super.visit (do not go inside)
    visit(ObjectCreationExpr) -> visit the arguments, NOT the anonymous body

  Here "forgetting super.visit" is used ON PURPOSE to skip lambdas.
  Same tool, opposite goal.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 EVIDENCE (real runs)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Fixture Complexity.java: 20 methods, each with "expected N" written by
  hand above it BEFORE the code existed. All 20 matched.

  Mutation checks:
    step into lambdas            -> lambda(boolean): expected 2 but was 4
    step into anonymous bodies   -> anonymous(): expected 1 but was 2
    count labels, not entries    -> grouped(String): expected 3 but was 4
  Each rule has a guard.

